NAME
       devd - the LikeOS device manager: lists disks, mounts drives and discs

SYNOPSIS
       devd [-f] [-v]

DESCRIPTION
       devd keeps the list of disks, partitions and USB drives, with the file
       system on each (FAT32, ext4, exFAT, NTFS, ISO 9660, swap), its label
       and UUID, and where it is mounted.  It follows the kernel's device
       events (/dev/devevents); without them it looks at /sys/block every
       two seconds.

       Programs talk to it through the socket /var/run/devd/socket: the
       desktop's volume agent (likeos-volume-agent), the file manager, and
       devctl(1).  Any local user may list the devices and follow their
       changes.  Mounting, unmounting, ejecting and formatting are done for
       root and for the user logged in at the console, and only as follows:

       USB drive       mount, unmount, eject, format (not when it is
                       write-protected)
       CD/DVD drive    mount (always read-only), unmount, eject (the tray
                       opens); never format
       built-in disk   mount and unmount its partitions; never eject, never
                       format.  The volume agent never mounts one by itself:
                       only when someone opens it in the file manager.

       A disk the running system uses -- anything on it mounted other than
       by devd, such as / or /boot/efi -- is a system disk: nothing is done
       with it at all, for root neither.  Neither are the EFI system,
       reserved, recovery and BIOS boot partitions of a built-in disk (read
       from its partition table).  When the mount table cannot be read,
       every built-in disk counts as a system disk.  devd never mounts
       anything on its own.

       A volume is mounted at /media/<user>/<label> (the UUID or the device
       name when it has no label), always nosuid and nodev; write-protected
       media, discs and ISO 9660 read-only.  FAT, exFAT and NTFS volumes
       belong to the user they are mounted for (files 0600, directories
       0700); an NTFS volume is never mounted with "force", so a volume
       Windows left hibernated is mounted read-only by the kernel.
       /media and /media/<user> belong to root and only root can write to
       them; the user may enter their own directory through an access ACL
       (or the directory is readable by everyone where no ACL can be set).
       When a drive is pulled out, or the disc or card in it is taken out
       or changed, its mounts are detached and their directories removed.

       A journal is never replayed without the user's consent.  When the
       journal of an ext4 volume, or the log of an NTFS volume, still holds
       changes that replaying it would write to the drive, the mount is
       refused with EUCLEAN ("ERR MOUNT EUCLEAN ...") and nothing is
       written.  The desktop then asks the user (likeos-journal-prompt);
       when the user agrees it sends "MOUNT <name> recover", and devd
       mounts the volume with the journal replayed.  devctl mount --recover
       does the same from a shell.  "recover" changes nothing for the other
       file systems.

       Labels are read from the volume: the root directory for FAT and
       exFAT, the $Volume record of the MFT for NTFS, the Joliet descriptor
       (else the primary one) for ISO 9660.

       Formatting runs mkfs.fat(1) for FAT32, mke2fs(1) for ext4,
       mkfs.exfat(1) for exFAT (no partition table, also on a whole drive)
       or mkntfs(1) for NTFS; a full format writes zeros over the whole
       volume first, then makes the file system.  A label is checked
       first: FAT32 11 characters, ext4 16 bytes, exFAT 11 and NTFS 128
       UTF-16 code units, without control characters or the characters
       the file system reserves.

OPTIONS
       -f     Stay in the foreground and log to standard error.  This is how
              init(1) starts it, from /etc/inittab: respawn /usr/sbin/devd -f .

       -v     Log debugging messages as well.

FILES
       /var/run/devd/socket   the socket clients connect to
       /dev/devevents         the kernel's device events
       /media                 where drives and discs are mounted

SEE ALSO
       devctl(1), mount(1), mkfs.fat(1), mke2fs(1), mkfs.exfat(1),
       mkntfs(1), init(1)

